    "result_text": "The build is green \u2014 all tests pass.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `locality` field, derived from the owner's city via the fixed table (Sydney\u2192NSW, Melbourne\u2192VIC, Brisbane\u2192QLD), returning `UNKNOWN` for any other city.\n\nChanges:\n1. **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a read-only `locality` property to the `Owner` response schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` carries the field.\n2. **`src/main/java/.../mapper/CityRegion.java`** (new) \u2014 a small helper holding the fixed city\u2192region map with a `locality(city)` lookup.\n3. **`src/main/java/.../mapper/OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 a `@Mapping` on `toOwnerDto` that populates `locality` from `owner.getCity()` via `CityRegion.locality(...)`.\n\n### One trap worth flagging\nMy first attempt put the `locality(String)` logic as a `default` method directly on `OwnerMapper`. That silently broke owner **creation**: MapStruct treats any `String \u2192 String` method on a mapper as a candidate mapping method and hijacked the string-field mappings in `toOwner(OwnerFieldsDto)`, so the E.164-normalized telephone never reached the entity and persistence failed with a 500 `ConstraintViolationException`. Moving the logic into the standalone `CityRegion` class (referenced by fully-qualified name in the expression) keeps it out of MapStruct's method-resolution scope and fixes it. The full suite is green.",
